// A NESTED call to an unconditionally-suspending GENERIC must actually PARK.
//
// `hop<T>` suspends unconditionally and calls no method on its type parameter,
// so its instantiation gets no per-instantiation effect node — and design
// 218b's landing note (c) records what follows: the coroutine transform
// DECLINES to promote it to a concrete spliced callee, and codegen's late
// monomorphization is what serves the call. Codegen has no frame machinery, so
// it compiles `hop$1$Int` as a plain function and the `yield_now()` inside it
// EVAPORATES — the emitted body is `ret i64 %x`.
//
// Nothing says so. The program compiles, prints the right numbers and never
// cedes, which contradicts the documented promise that suspending calls "embed
// at any nesting depth ... or error cleanly — never silently block".
//
// The oracle is INTERLEAVING, because that is the whole of what is lost. Two
// tasks in a single-threaded group take turns at every real suspension
// (deterministic by design), so a task whose only suspension was erased runs to
// completion before its sibling starts: today this prints `A 0 / A 1 / B 0 /
// B 1`. Write the same body with `yield_now()` DIRECTLY in it and the twin
// interleaves, which is what makes the expectation below a statement about the
// nested generic rather than about the scheduler.
//
// XFAIL: DF-258a — the nested unconditionally-suspending generic's yield is
// erased by codegen's late monomorphization. Design 218 unit 1.5 stage 4 flips
// it: phase 2 splices the instantiation as an ordinary concrete function whose
// own effect node says it suspends, and codegen can no longer instantiate
// anything.
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// A 0
// B 0
// A 1
// B 1

import std.task.*

func hop<T>(x: T) -> T {
    yield_now()
    x
}

func nested(tag: String) -> Int {
    var i = 0
    while i < 2 {
        let v = hop<Int>(i)
        print("{tag} {v}")
        i = i + 1
    }
    0
}

func main() {
    var g = TaskGroup()
    let a = g.spawn(nested("A"))
    let b = g.spawn(nested("B"))
    let _ = a.join()
    let _ = b.join()
}
